    "decision_details_body": "- tourism sector has been identified as one of the key sectors developing the country's economy.  There has been a significant increase in tourist arrivals immediately after the conflict situation in the country, with total arrivals surpassing 650,000, confirming the fact that Sri Lanka has been globally acclaimed as one of the finest destinations for tourists.  The above development strategy for 2011 to 2016, submitted by Hon. Basil Rajapaksa, Minister of Economic Development, which covers wider issues relating to the industry such as the government policy, capacity and manpower requirements, infrastructure development, improving service standards, marketing and promotions, public relations, tourist safety and domestic tourism etc., was approved by the Cabinet.  It has been estimated that by 2016 the industry is capable of creating 500,000 direct and indirect employment opportunities."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2011-07-27-005-en",
    "num": "5",
    "date_str": "2011-07-27",
    "description": "Establishment of a Resident Sri Lanka Mission in Ankara, Republic of Turkey",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=4101",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2011-07-27",
    "decision_details_body": "- Sri Lanka and Turkey enjoy long standing bilateral relations. Sri Lanka's bilateral trade with Turkey has recorded a 50% growth from US $97.431 million in 2005 to US $145.994 million in 2009. Turkey is one of the largest importers of Sri Lankan Tea. There is considerable potential for attracting more Turkish investments in fisheries, food processing, infrastructure development, electronics  and IT Sectors etc.  With the view to increase the relationship between the two countries, the proposal made by Hon. (Prof) G.L. Peiris, Minister of External Affairs to establish an Embassy in Ankara, in the Republic of Turkey, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
