  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-02-11-016-en",
    "num": "16",
    "date_str": "2015-02-11",
    "description": "Release of Land and  Property in the High Security Zone",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=5948",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-02-11",
    "decision_details_body": "- a large extent of land out of the 11,639 acres which was under the control of the the Security Forces during the period of conflict has been released by now and a land in extent of 6,152 acres continue to be under the control of the Sri Lanka Army and the Sri Lanka Air Force to ensure the security of the Palali area. The proposal made by His Excellency the President in his capacity as the Minister of Defence to release a further 1000 acres from this High Security Zone in stages and to release 220 acres situated in the Grama Niladhari Division of Valalai - J/284 in the Walikamam East Divisional Secretary's Division and to establish a model village to resettle 1022 families displaced due to the conflict was approved by the Cabinet.  Under this programme it is also proposed to provide a block of land in extent of 20 perches each and housing assistance and the model village so established to be provided with a school, preschool, hospital, places of religious observance, community centres and other infrastructure facilities.   Action will be pursued to release the remaining 780 acres expected to be released also for the future resettlement of the persons displaced due to the conflict. \r\n\r\n\tIt was also decided to release the land under the control of the Sri Lanka Air Force situated in Panama in the Eastern Province, except for the 25 acres where some buildings are being constructed, to enable it to be distributed among the landless persons of the area. \r\n\r\n\tIt was further decided to release the buildings in the High Security Zone in close proximity to the President's House in Colombo and also those around Temple Trees, presently under the control of the Security Forces."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-02-18-001-en",
    "num": "1",
    "date_str": "2015-02-18",
    "description": "Establishment of Food Security Operation Room",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=5949",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-02-18",
    "decision_details_body": "- The Cabinet noted that 'food security along with sustainable agriculture' is a priority sector identified in the Election Manifesto of His Excellency the President Maithripala Sirisena, and that Hon. Gamini Jayawickrema Perera, Minister of Food Security, has taken action to establish a \u201cFood Security Operation Room\u201d with the view to collecting, compiling and analyzing food production and imports related activities to regulate the market of the main food items through suitable policy initiatives.  The Cabinet further noted that it is expected that this will be the focal point for dissemination of information related to strategies adopted for the food security of the country."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-02-18-002-en",
    "num": "2",
    "date_str": "2015-02-18",
    "description": "Demographic Health Survey of Sri Lanka 2014/2015",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=5950",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-02-18",
    "decision_details_body": "- collection of current data is necessary in taking policy decisions for the advancement of the health sector, and for the purpose it has been planned to conduct the Demographic Health Survey of Sri Lanka - 2014/2015 in this year.  A survey of this nature, for the last time, had been conducted during the year 2006 and 2007 and the activities pertaining to the survey to be conducted this year will be carried out jointly by the Ministry of Health and Indigenous and the Department of Census and Statistics. The estimated cost for the survey is  Two Hundred and One Million rupees. The proposal made by Hon. (Dr.) Rajitha Senaratne, Minister of Health and Indigenous Medicine to conduct the above survey, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-02-18-003-en",
    "num": "3",
    "date_str": "2015-02-18",
    "description": "Lands of the Land Reform Commission on which housing schemes have been constructed by the National Housing Development Authority",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=5951",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-02-18",
    "decision_details_body": "- although the possession of 34 lands in extent of 271 Acres, 02 Roods and 38.4 Perches owned by the Land Reform Commission has been taken over by the National Housing Development Authority, the ownership had not been transferred over to the National Housing Development Authority and therefore, the residents of the 748 houses constructed by the National Housing Authority on these lands, have not gained the legal ownership of the houses. Therefore, Hon. Sajith Premadasa, Minister of Housing and Samurdhi had submitted a proposal to vest the said lands with the National Housing Development Authority with a view to granting legal ownership to the residents of the houses situated in 34 lands under the 100 Day Programme implemented by the Ministry of Housing and Samurdhi. Since the lands belonging to the Land Reform Commission cannot be vested on a free grant,  in terms of the Land Reforms Law, approval was granted by the Cabinet to vest the lands with the National Housing Development Authority at a most concessionary value after  conducting negotiations with the Land Reform Commission."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-02-18-004-en",
    "num": "4",
    "date_str": "2015-02-18",
    "description": "Children (Judicial Protection) Bill",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=5952",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-02-18",
    "decision_details_body": "- the Children and Young Persons Ordinance last amended in 1945, has not been subject to change for nearly 70 years and therefore, it has been difficult to ensure effective, speedy and expeditious disposal of cases.  At the same time,  a need has arisen to amend the existing laws in keeping with the many changes that have occurred in our legal system and as well as in the international sphere, with regard to Judicial Procedure. Further, with the ratification by Sri Lanka of the UN Convention on the Rights of the Children, amending the existing laws to address the shortcomings in both immediate and long term response to the care and protection of the children and the juvenile justice system has become necessary. Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. (Dr.) Wijayadasa Rajapaksa, Minister of Justice and Labour Relations, to instruct the Legal Draftsman to draft the proposed Childrens' (Judicial Protection) Bill, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
