    "num": "9",
    "date_str": "2015-09-09",
    "description": "Satellite for SAARC Countries to be launched by the Government of India",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=6281",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-09-09",
    "decision_details_body": "- Member States of the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation (SAARC) including Sri Lanka welcomed in principle, the offer made by India to develop and launch a Satellite, at the 18th SAARC summit held in Kathmandu, Nepal, in November 2014. India will design, build and launch the satellite as a gift from India to the SAARC Member States and in order to provide the services necessary to enhance the cooperation among the SAARC member countries, the Satellite will be located at 48\u00b0E.  In this backdrop, India expects to enter into specific Bilateral Agreements with each SAARC Member State, in regard to the proposed  Orbit Frequency Coordination of Satellite. As such, the  proposal made by  Hon. Mangala Samaraweera, the Minister of Foreign Affairs, to sign the proposed Bilateral Agreement between Sri Lanka and India, during the forthcoming Indian visit of the Hon. Prime Minister, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-09-09-010-en",
    "num": "10",
    "date_str": "2015-09-09",
    "description": "Common Programme of the National Government",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=6282",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-09-09",
    "decision_details_body": "- Hon. Prime Minister brought to the notice of the Cabinet, the necessity of the formulation of a Common Programme for the National Government, based on the proposals which are in the best interest of the country, included in the Election Manifestos submitted by the United National Party (UNP) and other political parties including the United People's Freedom Alliance (UPFA),  at the recently concluded general election, and that it is appropriate to conduct the future activities of the government accordingly. As such, the Cabinet agreed, that the said programme should be formulated under the leadership of H.E. the President and the Hon. Prime Minister."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-09-16-001-en",
    "num": "1",
    "date_str": "2015-09-16",
    "description": "Upholding the Collective Responsibility of the Cabinet of Ministers",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=6283",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-09-16",
    "decision_details_body": "- In terms of the Article 42(2) of the Constitution, the Cabinet of Ministers is  charged with the direction and control of the Government of the Republic of Sri Lanka and is collectively responsible and answerable to the Parliament.  However, having considered the various issues arisen due to the disregard of that hallowed tradition at certain instances and also the delays caused in implementing the decisions, which should have been implemented forthwith in the best interest of the country, the following proposals made by H.E. the President Maithripala Sirisena, with a view to preventing such occurrences in the future, was approved by the Cabinet. \n\n*\tthat a pledge should be made by each Member of the Cabinet of Ministers that he/she would strictly adhere to and exhibit his/her dedication to uphold and to establish a virtuous political culture newly, with due respect to the hallowed tradition of upholding the Collective Responsibility of the Cabinet of Ministers.    \n\n*\tappointment of a Ministerial Committee to formulate a Code of Ethics  in keeping with the best practices of the good governance to be followed by the Members of the Cabinet voluntarily and adoption of the Code of Ethics so formulated by the Cabinet within one month. \n\n\tAccordingly, all Ministers made a pledge at this meeting of the Cabinet of Ministers, to the effect that the Collective Responsibility of the Cabinet will be upheld."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-09-16-002-en",
    "num": "2",
    "date_str": "2015-09-16",
    "description": "Consent of Sri Lanka to Minimize the Impact of the Climate Change, in Keeping with the Kyoto Protocol",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=6284",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-09-16",
    "decision_details_body": "- The Protocol adopted in the year 1997 in Kyoto, Japan, by the parties that are legally bound to achieve the objectives of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), which is the major Convention collectively adopted by the International Community to discuss the issues related to climate change is known as Kyoto Protocol. \n\n\tAlthough, the Green House Gas Emissions by the industrialized countries were to be reduced by at least 5.2% against the gas level emitted by such countries in 1990, during the first commitment period of this Protocol, which implemented during the period of 2008 - 2012, but the target could not be achieved by the year 2012. Therefore, at the 8th meeting of the parties held in Doha-Qatar in the year 2012, a resolution was adopted to implement a Second Commitment Period by which it is anticipated to reduce the Green House Gas Emissions by the industrialized countries at least by 18% as against their emissions in 1990, individually or collectively, during the period of 2013-2020.  \n\n\tThere is a risk of facing adverse affects by the island nations like Sri Lanka due to situations such as rise of sea level occurred due to increase of global warming that may be  caused as a result of excessive emission of Green House Gases by the developed industrialized countries. \n\n\tThrough expressing concurrence for the Second Commitment Period of the Kyoto Protocol, it will be possible for the member countries like Sri Lanka to exert  the developed industrialized  countries to reduce their excessive emission of carbon and also to obtain foreign aids from the nation parties for the implementation of various projects in that regard. \n\n\tAs such, the proposal made by H.E. the President, Maithripala Sirisena in his capacity as the the Minister of Mahaweli Development and Environment, to express the consent of Sri Lanka to protect the Earth through further providing the required assistance for the proposed Second Commitment Period of the Kyoto Protocol, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-09-16-003-en",
    "num": "3",
    "date_str": "2015-09-16",
    "description": "Epilepsy Hospital and Healthcare Centres Project",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=6285",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-09-16",
    "decision_details_body": "- The Government of Sri Lanka with the assistance of the Saudi Fund for Development, commenced the construction of  a Modern State of the Art Hospital for the treatment of the Epileptic patients in Sri Lanka in the year 2008. The implementation of this project was somewhat delayed due to various reasons and by now 90.5% of the project has been completed.  The hospital is due to be opened for clinical purposes in December, 2015. The proposal made by Hon. Ravi Karunanayake, the Minister of Finance, to obtain the additional sum of about US$ 12 million  further required for the purpose from the Saudi Fund for Development, for providing the necessary enhanced facilities to render a greater service to the people, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2015-09-16-004-en",
    "num": "4",
    "date_str": "2015-09-16",
    "description": "Amendment to the Registration of Deaths (Temporary Provisions) Act, No.19 of 2010",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=6286",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2015-09-16",
    "decision_details_body": "- In terms of the provisions of Births and Deaths Registration Act, No.17 of 1951, the authority to register Deaths has been vested  with the Registrar General's Department.  However, as the Act does not provide provisions for the registration of Deaths occurred due to unnatural circumstances such as terrorist activities, civil riots or natural disasters and where the dead bodies of persons reported missing cannot be found, the Registration of Deaths (Temporary Provisions) Act, No.19 of 2010 was introduced as a solution to that impediment. Nevertheless, at instances where the relatives of a missing person do not accept the death of  that missing person due to some reason or other, it is impossible to register such disappearances as deaths under the above Act. It is observed that family members of missing person could not obtain any of their due entitlements in such instances.  As such, the proposal made by Hon. Vajira Abeywardena, the Minister of Home Affairs, to amend the Registration of Deaths (Temporary Provisions) Act, No.19 of 2010 to enable to issue a Certificate similar to that of a Certificate of Absence issued by many countries in respect of the missing person / a person suspected to be dead, in line with the internationally accepted practice, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
