    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2016-03-02",
    "decision_details_body": "- Consultations on executing a new bilateral Air Services Agreement between Sri\tLanka and Austria  were held in the month of November, 2015 in Vienna, Austria, with the participation of the aeronautical authorities\tof both countries. Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. Nimal\t Siripala De Silva, the Minister of Transport and Civil Aviation, to sign the new bilateral Air Services Agreement between Sri Lanka and Austria by an accredited representative of Sri Lanka, as agreed upon at the above consultations and to take appropriate measures thereafter for the effective implementation of the same, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2016-03-02-004-en",
    "num": "4",
    "date_str": "2016-03-02",
    "description": "Accession to the Ottawa Convention/Anti-Personnel Mine Ban Convention by Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=6590",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2016-03-02",
    "decision_details_body": "- Due to the conflict situation that prevailed in Sri Lanka, the Northern and Eastern provinces of the country have been adversely affected by the landmines and explosive remnants of war. Mine clearance operations are vital for the safe resettlement of internally displaced persons (IDP), and the threats caused by the landmines and the explosive remnants of war need to be prevented for the better living conditions of the people residing in those areas and infrastructure development activities. Therefore, the joint proposal made by Hon. Mangala Samaraweera, the Minister of Foreign Affairs and Hon. D.M.\tSwaminathan, the Minister of Prison Reforms, Rehabilitation, Resettlement and Hindu Religious Affairs, to accede to the Anti-Personnel Mine Ban Convention which is also known as Ottawa Convention, by the Government of Sri Lanka, which arranges the required background for\tthe purpose, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
