  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2020-10-19-008-en",
    "num": "8",
    "date_str": "2020-10-19",
    "description": "National policy and strategies on traditional knowledge and practices related to biodiversity",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=10681",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2020-10-19",
    "decision_details_body": "-Sri Lanka has signed the Biodiversity Convention in 1992,with the objective of conserving biodiversity, sustainable use and equitable sharing of its benefits. According to the provisions of the said Convention, the Government is committed to take action to deal with the cultural diversity related to biodiversity, the indigenous people and their knowledge, its products and practices and to take measures to preserve them.\n\nAccordingly, the National Policy and Strategies on Traditional Knowledge and Practices related to Biodiversity prepared by obtaining the comments and suggestions from the public with the contribution of all relevant stakeholders and under the guidance of the National Experts Committee on Indigenous Knowledge and Practices established under the Ministry of Environment was submitted to the Cabinet by the Minister of Environment and the Cabinet granted approval for the same."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2020-10-19-009-en",
    "num": "9",
    "date_str": "2020-10-19",
    "description": "Single Use Plastic and Polythene Waste Management",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=10682",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2020-10-19",
    "decision_details_body": "-Regulations such as banning the production of polythene of 20 microns or below, banning the manufacture of polythene food wrappers, and banning the open burning of polythene in the open environment have been promulgated with effect from 2017\u201109\u201101, in order to minimize the environmental damage caused by polythene and plastics within Sri Lanka and steps have also been taken by the Central Environmental Authority, to promote other alternatives instead of the banned polythene while the said regulations are in force.\n\t\n\tIn accordance with the consensus arrived at the discussions held with the relevant stakeholder institutions on Polythene and Plastic Management, the proposal made by the Minister of Environment, to ban the following Polythene and Plastic products in order to minimize single use Polythene and Plastic products with effect from 2021\u201101\u201101 and introduce other alternatives instead, was approved by the Cabinet.\n\n*\tProhibition of wrapping of chemicals or insecticides in Poly Ethylene Terephthalate (PET) and Poly Vinyl Chloride (PVC) containers and to use containers made of glass or other raw materials as an alternative.\n\n*\tProhibition of sachets made of polythene and plastic less than 20 ml / 20 g (other than for the use of packing food and medicine) and use containers of 100 ml/ 100 g or bigger as an alternative.\n\n*\tProhibition of various inflatable toys made out of plastic (excluding balloons, balls and water floating toys) and use the toys made of Eco-friendly and hygienic raw material as an alternative.\n*\tProhibition of plastic cotton buds (excluding materials used for medical treatments) and use cotton buds made from biodegradable materials, as an alternative.\n\n*\tMandating to indicate the 1-7 Codes for identification of internationally prescribed plastic to promote the recycling of all plastic products."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2020-10-19-010-en",
    "num": "10",
    "date_str": "2020-10-19",
    "description": "Re-determination of the interest rate for all loan schemes of the Smallholder Agribusiness Partnership Programme (SAPP) and expansion of relief for partner/promoter companies of Agricultural Value Chain Project Development - Smallholder Agribusiness Partnerships Programme",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=10683",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2020-10-19",
    "decision_details_body": "-The Cabinet noted the following matters submitted by the Minister of Agriculture to implement the Smallholder Agribusiness Partnership Programme, operating under the funds of the International Fund for Agriculture Development (IFAD) and the Government of Sri Lanka, in the manner stated below by further expanding the loan facilities granted at present under the programme in terms of the decision taken by the National Steering Committee of the aforesaid Programme.\n\n    \u2022 To revise the annual interest rate for all loans granted under the Smallholder \tAgribusiness Partnership Programme to 6.5% with immediate effect.\n      \n    \u2022 To provide adjustment credit facility for partner/promoter companies of Agricultural Value Chain Project Development subject to a maximum of US$ \t100,000 under the annual interest rate of 6.5%.\n      \n    \u2022 To increase the adjustment credit amount granted to partner/promoter companies of Agricultural Value Chain Project Development from US$ 50,000 to US$100,000."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2020-10-19-011-en",
    "num": "11",
    "date_str": "2020-10-19",
    "description": "Complete Re-organization of the Colombo Flight Information Region of Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=10684",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2020-10-19",
    "decision_details_body": "-The Colombo Flight Information Region is nearly 25 times of the total land area of Sri Lanka. The Airport and Aviation Services (Sri Lanka) Limited is responsible for providing quality air navigational services within the Flight Information Region. Accordingly, Sri Lanka will have to maintain the services required to meet the projected capacity demands in the Asia-Pacific Region, as well as to maintain the air traffic services, aviation procedures and related infrastructure facilities in line with the standards set forth by the International Civil Aviation Organization, in the future. \n\nTherefore, the existing air route structure within the Colombo Flight Information Region needs to be restructured immediately so as to suit to the present day requirements and it should be formulated in line with the \"Seamless Air Navigational Services Concept\" of the International Civil Aviation Organization. Accordingly, the proposal made by the Minister of Tourism, to fully reorganize the Colombo Flight Information Region subsequent to a study conducted in that regard, enabling to completely reorganize the Flight Information Region of Sri Lanka and to obtain the services of an integrated specialist consultancy service for the said study, was approved by the Cabinet ."
  },
